/vi/XmjLpePIz8o/hqdefault.jpg?sqp=-oaymwEjCPYBEIoBSFryq4qpAxUIARUAAAAAGAElAADIQj0AgKJDeAE=&rs=AOn4CLBzCrXh5YZdc55rqHB1zfvwE_Bnjw", "height": 138, "width": 246, "preference": -7, "id": "32", "resolution": "246x138"}, {"url": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi/XmjLpePIz8o/hqdefault.jpg?sqp=-oaymwEjCNACELwBSFryq4qpAxUIARUAAAAAGAElAADIQj0AgKJDeAE=&rs=AOn4CLAFlNDxbcNghnQoxcplPSR5rMnWjg", "height": 188, "width": 336, "preference": -7, "id": "33", "resolution": "336x188"}, {"url": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi/XmjLpePIz8o/hqdefault.jpg", "height": 360, "width": 480, "preference": -7, "id": "34", "resolution": "480x360"}, {"url": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi_webp/XmjLpePIz8o/hqdefault.webp", "preference": -6, "id": "35"}, {"url": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi/XmjLpePIz8o/sddefault.jpg", "preference": -5, "id": "36"}, {"url": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi_webp/XmjLpePIz8o/sddefault.webp", "preference": -4, "id": "37"}, {"url": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi/XmjLpePIz8o/hq720.jpg", "preference": -3, "id": "38"}, {"url": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi_webp/XmjLpePIz8o/hq720.webp", "preference": -2, "id": "39"}, {"url": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi/XmjLpePIz8o/maxresdefault.jpg", "preference": -1, "id": "40"}, {"url": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi_webp/XmjLpePIz8o/maxresdefault.webp", "preference": 0, "id": "41"}], "thumbnail": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi/XmjLpePIz8o/hqdefault.jpg", "description": "𝐂𝐚𝐫𝐥 𝐂𝐚𝐫𝐥𝐭𝐨𝐧 (𝐌𝐚𝐲 𝟐𝟏, 𝟏𝟗𝟓𝟑 – 𝐃𝐞𝐜𝐞𝐦𝐛𝐞𝐫 𝟏𝟒, 𝟐𝟎𝟐𝟓) ⁣𝐂𝐚𝐫𝐥 𝐂𝐚𝐫𝐥𝐭𝐨𝐧 𝐝𝐢𝐝𝐧’𝐭 𝐣𝐮𝐬𝐭 𝐬𝐢𝐧𝐠 𝐚𝐛𝐨𝐮𝐭 𝐞𝐯𝐞𝐫𝐥𝐚𝐬𝐭𝐢𝐧𝐠 𝐥𝐨𝐯𝐞 — 𝐡𝐞 𝐥𝐢𝐯𝐞𝐝 𝐢𝐭 𝐭𝐡𝐫𝐨𝐮𝐠𝐡 𝐡𝐢𝐬 𝐯𝐨𝐢𝐜𝐞. 𝐋𝐨𝐧𝐠 𝐚𝐟𝐭𝐞𝐫 𝐭𝐡𝐞 𝐦𝐮𝐬𝐢𝐜 𝐟𝐚𝐝𝐞𝐬, 𝐭𝐡𝐞 𝐰𝐚𝐫𝐦𝐭𝐡, 𝐬𝐨𝐮𝐥 𝐚𝐧𝐝 𝐡𝐮𝐦𝐚𝐧𝐢𝐭𝐲 𝐢𝐧 𝐡𝐢𝐬 𝐬𝐨𝐧𝐠𝐬 𝐰𝐢𝐥𝐥 𝐜𝐨𝐧𝐭𝐢𝐧𝐮𝐞 𝐭𝐨 𝐫𝐞𝐦𝐢𝐧𝐝 𝐮𝐬 𝐡𝐨𝐰 𝐝𝐞𝐞𝐩𝐥𝐲 𝐨𝐧𝐞 𝐯𝐨𝐢𝐜𝐞 𝐜𝐚𝐧 𝐭𝐨𝐮𝐜𝐡 𝐭𝐡𝐞 𝐰𝐨𝐫𝐥𝐝. Album: Everlasting Love  -  Released in 1974. Songwriters: Mac Gayden and Buzz Cason [Gayden died April 16, 2025, from complications of Parkinson's disease; age of 83 and Cason died June 16, 2024: age of 84]. The original version of \"Everlasting Love\" was recorded in Nashville by Robert Knight, whose producers/writers Buzz Cason and Mac Gayden aimed to record him in a Motown style. Ultimately \"Everlasting Love\" was released as an A-side for Knight and reached #13 in 1967. Subsequently, the song has reached the US Top 40 three times, most successfully by Carl Carlton, who peaked at #6 in 1974.  Carl Carlton is an American artist who sings R&B, soul and funk. Born in Detroit, Michigan on May 21 in 1953, he started his career during the 1960s when he was still a kid. In 1968, he signed a contract with Back Beat Records, after which he moved to Texas.. In the early 70’s music era he achieved his biggest hit with “Everlasting Love” which became a Top 10 pop hit single and oldies music classic.                                                                                                                                         \n\nPresenter of Soul Train: Donald Cortez (Don) Cornelius (Chicago (Illinois), September 27, 1936 - Sherman Oaks (California), died: February 1, 2012). Cornelius was found dead in his Los Angeles home at the age of 75. The presenter was found to have succumbed to a gunshot wound to the head as a result of a suicide attempt. Below: Continuation of health problems and depression Don Cornelius.\n\nThe immense popularity of the song lies in its flexibility; it could be adapted to various genres, from soul and pop to rock, country, and dance. Also covered by :\nU2, Jamie Cullum,The Love Affair, The Ravers, MFP Studio Artists, Hank Locklin, James Last, David Ruffin ,The Senate, The Sonic, Kings Road, The Hiltonaires, Solomon Burke, Patricia Paay, Louise Mandrell, Narvel Felts, Soirée, The Kendalls, Rex Smith and Rachel Sweet, The Truth, Sandra (Germany), David Essex, Worlds Apart, Gloria Estefan, Stanford Harmonics, Seventh Avenue Party Animals, The Cast from Casualty, Reverse Osmosis, På Slaget 12, Mysterio Scooter, Kerry Norton, Michael Ball, The Soldiers, Manuel, etc. \n\nLYRICS:\nOpen up your eyes, then you'll realize here I stand\nWith my everlasting love.\nNeed you by my side, girl you'll be my bride.\nYou'll never be denied everlasting love.\nFrom the very start, open up your heart,\nBe a lasting part of everlasting love.\nOoh, ooh.-- Real love will last forever\n\nHearts go astray, leaving hurt when they go.\nI went away just when you needed me so. Oh yeah\nFilled with regret I come back beggin' you,\nForgive, forget. Where's the love we once knew?\n\nOpen up your eyes, then you'll realize here I stand\nWith my everlasting love.\nNeed you by my side, girl you'll be my bride.\nYou'll never be denied everlasting love.\nFrom the very start, open up your heart,\nBe a lasting part of everlasting love.\nReal love will last forever.\nOoh, ooh. Yeah, yeah\n\nWhere life's river flows, no one really knows\n'til someone's there to show the way to lasting love.\nLike the sun that shines, endlessly it shines,\nYou always will be mine. It's eternal love.\nWhen other loves are gone, ours will still be strong,\nWe have our very own everlasting love.\nReal love will last forever.\n\nCornelius had been struggling for years with serious health issues. In 1982, he underwent a 21-hour brain surgery to correct a congenital abnormality in blood vessels of his brain. After this procedure, he continued to suffer from persistent health problems, including epileptic seizures, which worsened during the last six months of his life. This physical decline led to a deep depression. According to the autopsy report, he called his son early in the morning on the day of his death and said, “I don’t know how much longer I can take this.”\n\nPersonal Struggles\nIn addition to his health problems, Cornelius faced personal setbacks. In 2008, he was arrested for domestic violence against his then-wife, which led to a conviction and three years’ probation. During the divorce proceedings in 2009, he stated that he was suffering from \"significant health issues\" and expressed a desire to finalize the divorce before he died.\n\nA Reclusive Life\nAccording to his son, Tony Cornelius, Don was a very private person who often kept his feelings to himself. Although his family knew he was struggling, they didn’t realize how deep his depression really was. \n\nCultural Legacy\nThrough Soul Train he provided a national platform for African American artists. His iconic sign-off at the end of each episode—“Love, peace, and soul”—remains a powerful reflection of his impact on music and television history.", "channel_id": "UCkuVZ20MMjqVKesSqp6gwLg", "channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCkuVZ20MMjqVKesSqp6gwLg", "duration": 208, "view_count": 1352545, "age_limit": 0, "webpage_url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XmjLpePIz8o", "categories": ["Music"], "tags": ["Carl Carlton", "everlasting love", "romance", "love", "classic", "70's", "cover", "Knight", "bride", "disco", "soul", "lyrics", "gloria esthefan", "U2", "old school", "golden oldie", "golden", "oldies", "music", "The Bridget Jone's Diary movie", "The Bridget Jone's Diary", "soul train"], "playable_in_embed": true, "live_status": "not_live", "media_type": "video", "_format_sort_fields": ["quality", "res", "fps", "hdr:12", "source", "vcodec", "channels", "acodec", "lang", "proto"], "automatic_captions": {}, "subtitles": {}, "comment_count": 1300, "like_count": 21899, "channel": "MrHaagsesjonny1", "channel_follower_count": 109000, "uploader": "MrHaagsesjonny1", "uploader_id": "@MrHaagsesjonny1", "uploader_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@MrHaagsesjonny1", "upload_date": "20180408", "timestamp": 1523195210, "availability": "public", "webpage_url_basename": "watch", "webpage_url_domain": "youtube.com", "extractor": "youtube", "extractor_key": "Youtube", "display_id": "XmjLpePIz8o", "fulltitle": "𝐄𝐕𝐄𝐑𝐋𝐀𝐒𝐓𝐈𝐍𝐆 𝐋𝐎𝐕𝐄 - 𝐂𝐚𝐫𝐥 𝐂𝐚𝐫𝐥𝐭𝐨𝐧 - @ 𝐒𝐨𝐮𝐥 𝐓𝐫𝐚𝐢𝐧 - 𝐑.𝐈.𝐏. 𝐂𝐚𝐫𝐥 𝐂𝐚𝐫𝐥𝐭𝐨𝐧 (𝐌𝐚𝐲 𝟐𝟏, 𝟏𝟗𝟓𝟑–𝐃𝐞𝐜.𝟏𝟒, 𝟐𝟎𝟐𝟓) HD", "duration_string": "3:28", "is_live": false, "was_live": false, "epoch": 1788320068, "format_id": "95", "url": "https://manifest.googlevideo.com/api/manifest/hls_playlist/expire/1788341665/ei/QZmXaormBpLQobIP8oahsQU/ip/18.229.136.40/i